{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_82","type":"agent_message","text":"The visual review caught a clipped title on portrait phones. I’ve resized that line, removed an overlapping caption, and added edge arrows for rescue beacons and gates outside the view. I also preserved combat stats across checkpoint loads and made controller focus visible on sliders. The final run will cover these changes and the packaged credits.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_83","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\np=Path('../exit-interview.json');d=json.loads(p.read_text())\nd['testingFeedback']=[\n'Three issues found and corrected: software WebGPU setup failure, portrait title clipping, and an incorrect frame-identity assertion in the test harness. Five stylistic lint findings were also corrected.',\n'One blocked rendering in the original software WebGPU test configuration. The corrected launch configuration passes GPU pixel readback, and forced device loss recovers to WebGL2. No unresolved critical issue was found.',\n'GPU pixel readback was the most helpful: state-only checks could not reveal that the headless canvas was failing to present the scene. The full real-physics campaign playthrough also verified every mission objective.',\n'Headless WebGPU screenshots were least helpful because that canvas can be blank. WebGL2 screenshots were useful for TV and portrait-phone layout review.'\n]\nd['engineFeedback']=[\n'TSL materials, procedural geometry and native AnimationMixer clips made it possible to build a consistent ink-and-cel world without downloaded art.',\n'Yes. I initially used software Vulkan flags that lost the WebGPU device. I isolated the configuration with GPU readback, corrected the launch flags, and implemented and tested renderer recovery.',\n'The flight and mission framework: combat, tractor recovery, docking, gate navigation, boss rules, controller-first menus, procedural audio and ghost-network adapters.',\n'Coordinating WebGPU initialization, asynchronous pixel readback, device-loss recovery and a WebGL2 path using the same TSL materials.',\n'none; application code configures and recovers the renderer, with no engine source modifications.',\n'A documented, complete device-loss recovery example that preserves a live scene while changing renderer backends.'\n]\nd['sourceInformation']['testing']='Headless Chromium 153; software WebGPU pixel readback and forced device-loss recovery; WebGL2 visual review at 1920×1080 and portrait phone sizes; full real-physics campaign playthrough; keyboard, emulated standard gamepad and multitouch; Web Audio analyser samples; two-page WebRTC; official AI Hub harness at 2 and 4 seats, including 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter, 5% packet loss and host departure. Physical controller, real mobile hardware, subjective listening and restrictive-NAT connectivity: unknown.'\np.write_text(json.dumps(d,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\\\\n')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_83","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\np=Path('../exit-interview.json');d=json.loads(p.read_text())\nd['testingFeedback']=[\n'Three issues found and corrected: software WebGPU setup failure, portrait title clipping, and an incorrect frame-identity assertion in the test harness. Five stylistic lint findings were also corrected.',\n'One blocked rendering in the original software WebGPU test configuration. The corrected launch configuration passes GPU pixel readback, and forced device loss recovers to WebGL2. No unresolved critical issue was found.',\n'GPU pixel readback was the most helpful: state-only checks could not reveal that the headless canvas was failing to present the scene. The full real-physics campaign playthrough also verified every mission objective.',\n'Headless WebGPU screenshots were least helpful because that canvas can be blank. WebGL2 screenshots were useful for TV and portrait-phone layout review.'\n]\nd['engineFeedback']=[\n'TSL materials, procedural geometry and native AnimationMixer clips made it possible to build a consistent ink-and-cel world without downloaded art.',\n'Yes. I initially used software Vulkan flags that lost the WebGPU device. I isolated the configuration with GPU readback, corrected the launch flags, and implemented and tested renderer recovery.',\n'The flight and mission framework: combat, tractor recovery, docking, gate navigation, boss rules, controller-first menus, procedural audio and ghost-network adapters.',\n'Coordinating WebGPU initialization, asynchronous pixel readback, device-loss recovery and a WebGL2 path using the same TSL materials.',\n'none; application code configures and recovers the renderer, with no engine source modifications.',\n'A documented, complete device-loss recovery example that preserves a live scene while changing renderer backends.'\n]\nd['sourceInformation']['testing']='Headless Chromium 153; software WebGPU pixel readback and forced device-loss recovery; WebGL2 visual review at 1920×1080 and portrait phone sizes; full real-physics campaign playthrough; keyboard, emulated standard gamepad and multitouch; Web Audio analyser samples; two-page WebRTC; official AI Hub harness at 2 and 4 seats, including 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter, 5% packet loss and host departure. Physical controller, real mobile hardware, subjective listening and restrictive-NAT connectivity: unknown.'\np.write_text(json.dumps(d,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\\\\n')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
